  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 245 AM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Widespread showers and thunderstorms are expected \nthis afternoon and evening with better potential for severe weather. \nThe main threat is damaging wind gusts with large hail and a couple \nof tornadoes also possible.\n\nAn anomalously strong upper trough and sfc low are progged to deepen \nover the Northeast CONUS today while we remain under NW flow aloft. \nThe deepening has already started, with decent height falls being \nobserved from the Great Lakes to Northeast over the past few hours. \nAt the surface, low pressure is developing along a cold front over \nthe eastern Great Lakes. Convection has developed over western and \ncentral Ohio and is organizing into an MCS. Will need to watch this \nconvection later this morning, but it appears that it will pass by \njust far enough to our W/SW as to not inhibit sfc heating (and thus \ninstability) in a meaningful way for at least the eastern 2/3 of the \narea. There is a low chc of late AM/midday showers in the Piedmont \nfrom this feature. The surface low will continue to deepen over the \nNortheast today, and in fact, could deepen to 995mb with -2 to -3 \nsigma height anomalies over the northern Mid-Atlantic/Northeast. The \nsynoptic features are impressive especially considering it is late \nJuly. There will be a belt of 35-45 kt 500mb winds on the SW side of \nthe trough that is progged move over the local area during the \nafternoon. The above mentioned cold front will move toward the area \nlater today and will cross the area tonight. Since the upstream MCS \nactivity should pass far enough to our W/SW, temperatures will be \nable to climb into the upper 80s in most areas by the afternoon with \nlower-mid 70s dew pts. This, coupled with solid 700-500mb lapse \nrates around 7.0 degrees C, should allow MLCAPE to rise to 2000-2500 \nJ/kg across much of the area. \n\nWhile there are slight differences in the CAMs with respect to exact \ntiming, convective mode, and convective evolution...the general \nconsensus is for numerous tstms to initially develop just to our \nnorth during the early-mid aftn before moving through the area from \nNW-SE during the late aftn-evening. The most likely timing for \nstorms is from 3-7 PM N/NW, 5-8 PM from south central VA to Richmond \nto the MD eastern Shore, and from 6-11 PM in SE VA/NE NC. Whether \nthe storms consolidate into a line or remain more discrete is not \nknown. The main threat appears to be damaging wind gusts, which \nwould be fairly robust whether storms remain more discrete or \nconsolidate into a line/line segments. There is also a large hail \nthreat, mainly across the NW 2/3 of the area where storms are more \nlikely to be discrete. Lastly, a couple of brief tornadoes cannot be \nruled out, especially closer to the coast where the winds will be \nmore backed. SPC has maintained a Slight Risk (level 2/5) for the \nentire area, with 30% wind probs and a 5% tornado risk for much of \nthe area. The main bust potential will be that the developing MCS \nover OH becomes stronger and tracks farther NE than expected. If \nthis happens, it could potentially contaminate our environment and \nlead to a lower than anticipated severe threat later today. Storms \nwill be moving quickly, which will act to limit flooding, but cannot \nrule out localized flash flooding, mainly in urban/flood prone \nareas. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Isolated showers and storms are possible on \nWednesday, with mainly dry weather to end the week. Temperatures \ndrop to slightly below average late this week.\n\nThere is some uncertainty in regards to rain chances in the wake of \nthe cold front on Wednesday. With the closer proximity of the upper \nlow, there cold be some showers on Wednesday, but guidance has \ntrended down on this. Have maintained slight chance PoPs across the \nnorthern portion of the forecast area for consistency. Thursday \nthrough Friday looks mostly dry, with near to slightly below average \ntemperatures, as LLVL flow will continue to be out of the NW. Tstm \nchances may return by late this weekend.",
